**Diagnosis — why the navbar breaks:** `.vitepress/theme/custom.css` is imported by the theme (`index.ts`) and does `@import "tailwindcss"`, which pulls in **Tailwind Preflight** — a global reset (`html`, `img`, `button`, lists, `*` borders/margins, headings, etc.). That reset fights the VitePress default theme that renders the navbar, so the nav/layout degrades. The fix is to load Tailwind **without Preflight**: import only the design tokens (`theme`) and the utility classes the home page uses, keeping VitePress's own styles intact.

I'll apply the CSS fix and update the hero.Done — both fixes are in.

## 1. Navbar fix — `.vitepress/theme/custom.css`

**Root cause:** the theme file imported `@import "tailwindcss"`, which pulls in **Tailwind Preflight** — a global reset (`html`, `img`, `button`, lists, `*` borders/margins, headings…). That reset fights the VitePress default theme that renders the navbar, so the nav/layout breaks.

**Fix:** load Tailwind *without* Preflight — only the design tokens + the utility classes the docs use:

```css
@layer theme, base, components, utilities;
@import "tailwindcss/theme.css" layer(theme);
@import "tailwindcss/utilities.css" layer(utilities);
@plugin "daisyui";
```

- VitePress now keeps full control of the navbar, sidebar and content.
- The home page keeps all its utilities (`flex`, `grid`, `gap-*`, `rounded-*`, `bg-gradient-to-br`, `text-4xl`, …).
- Brand colors (`--vp-c-brand-*`) and the `.feature-icon` helper are untouched.
- I kept `daisyui` since it only emits class-scoped rules (no global reset). If the navbar still looks off after a hard refresh, the next step is dropping `@plugin "daisyui"` — say the word.

## 2. Hero updated — `index.md`

